Falsifies `system_tests::user::revocation_window_tests::a_revoke_inside_a_send_cap_leaves_the_receiver_no_copy`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is the tree as it stood until 2026-10-04 (UTC): the patch puts `SEND_CAP` back to
reading its source with `sched::current_cap` in the syscall layer and handing the copy to
`sched::ipc_send_cap`, which files it under a second hold of `IPC_TABLES`. A `PageFrame::REVOKE`
that runs between the two deletes the source and every other copy it can see, and the copy is then
parked in `outgoing_cap` after the sweep has passed, so the receiver collects it.

This was the live tree, not an invented break. Milestone 761 (capability lookup off the global
lock) recorded the gap unverified in its `BUGS`; this test is what verified it.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!matches!(delivered, Some(c) if c.object == Object::PageFrame(phys, page_frame_run_len(1))), "a PageFrame revoked while its SEND_CAP was in progress reached the receiver ...")`. Confirmed red on riscv64 on 2026-10-04 (UTC), before the fix existed, and replayed red the same
day against the fixed tree at `revocation_window_tests.rs:165`, the assertion predicted.

**The assertions above the headline stay green under this patch**, which is what makes the red mean
the claim rather than the fixture: the vacuity guard says the thread really was held inside its
delegation, and the premise check says the sweep really did reach that thread's own table.

The patch leaves the aborted-send path (§101 (notification objects) ruling B) in its context
untouched. Nothing here is architecture-specific: the syscall layer, the sweeps and the seam are portable
code, so one patch serves every leg.

Re-cut on 2026-10-12 (UTC) by lane maintainer/long-function-paydown-invoke, when the rendezvous
arms of `syscall::invoke` moved to `kernel/src/syscall/rendezvous.rs` for milestone 888 (a ratchet
on function length); the defect is unchanged. Replayed red at the same assertion on aarch64 that
day.

